This is a "plugin" for the Video Disk Recorder (VDR).

Project's homepage:          https://github.com/FireFlyVDR/vdr-plugin-systeminfo
Latest version available at: https://github.com/FireFlyVDR/vdr-plugin-systeminfo/releases

See the file COPYING for license information.


Description:
------------
This plugin for Klaus Schmidinger's Video Disc Recorder VDR displays system
information like CPU type and speed, disk and swap space etc. It uses the output
of an external script (vdr-systeminfo.sh) which can be freely customized to the
personal needs.


Installation
------------
The systeminfo plugin is compiled and installed the same way as any other plugin:
- cd <VDR>/PLUGINS/src
- tar xf vdr-systeminfo-<version>.tar.gz
- ln -s systeminfo-<version> systeminfo
- cd ../..
- make plugins

Adapt the example script vdr-systeminfo.sh in the subfolder "scripts" to your needs
and configuration, copy it e.g. to the default path /usr/local/bin and verify that
it is execuatable for VDR. Alternatively, you could copy the adapted script to
any location you like and pass the location and name of the script as parameter to the
plugin:

  -s SCRIPT, --script=SCRIPT   optional name and path of systeminfo script
                               (default: '/usr/local/bin/vdr-systeminfo.sh')

If you are using VDR's conf.d mechanism you can also use the provided file
50-systeminfo.conf.


Configuration
--------------
The script vdr-systeminfo.sh is provided as an example and needs to be customized to your
system to get useful results as there is no general standard and systems vary a lot.
However, the provided vdr-systeminfo.sh contains already many examples which you can
adapt to your system.

The script is called continuously by the plugin with incremented numbers starting with
'1' until an empty line is returned.
The basic idea behind the format is that a script produces a name/value pair separated
by a tab (\t) for each line:
      name \t value

The blanks around the tab are for only better readability here and not in the script.
As many values do not occupy a complete line in OSD, you can put two values in one line:
      name \t value1 \t value2

The screen is devided in three columns for that purpose.

If a line looks like "name \t value1 value2" or "name \t value1 kB value2 kB" as e.g.
displayed by the 'df' command (note the blank(s) between the values instead of tab) the
plugin interprets value1 as "total" and value2 as "free" kilobytes and converts them to
MB or GB. These converted values are written in the 2nd column and a progress bar is
drawn in the third column showing "used of total" percentage.

If the special keyword "CPU%" is found in the second column, it will be replaced by the
actual cpu usage (which is too complex to caclulate in a script and therefore implemented
in the plugin).

If in front of the already mentionend strings an additional "s\t" appears, this entry is
considered 'static' which means that it will only be evaluated during the first display
cycle. This reduces the load of the machine as for example cpu type, distribution and
kernel version are very unlikely to change during the display period and therefore are
only evaluated once.
The time between two display updates can be modified in the plugin's settings and there's
also an entry to select if the display should be closed automatically after a selectable
amount of seconds or not.

You can test any line of the script by executing on the command line:
      vdr-systeminfo.sh <line number>
where <line number> is a valid number in the script. Be sure to execute it as the same
user VDR is running under to check the successful execution of external commands.

To show all result lines while configuring or debugging the script at command line you
can simply call the script with parameter 'a' or 'all' and it will output all lines until
an empty line is discovered.


Setup Options:
--------------
The following settings are available in the setup->plugins->systeminfo setup menu:
- Refresh intervall (s):     time between two refreshs of the display (1-20 seconds)
- Close display after (s):   time in seconds until the display closes automatically.
                             A value of 'never' disables the automatic close


